.TH PYSEMBR 1 "October 6, 2026" "pysembr 2.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pysembr \- Markdown-aware semantic line breaking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBpysembr\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pysembr
reads Markdown text from standard input or a file, joins soft-wrapped prose,
and writes formatted text to standard output or a file. It splits sentences
and, according to the selected mode, splits long sentences at punctuation or
language-specific words. Input is always treated as Markdown, including files
whose names end in
.BR .txt .
Formatting follows Semantic Line Breaks approximately.
.PP
The default width is 75 Unicode code points in emitted source. It counts list
indentation, markers, inline markup, and preserved hard-break markers. Equality
fits. Width is a soft limit in punctuation and words modes: protected content,
Markdown safety, or the absence of a usable boundary can leave a line longer
than the target. There is no arbitrary whitespace or word breaking.
.SS Splitting modes
The modes are cumulative. Sentence boundaries remain enabled in every mode.
.TP
.B sentences
Split at recognized sentence boundaries only. Width does not subdivide a
sentence. Hard breaks and document structure still preserve their boundaries.
.TP
.B punctuation
Includes
.B sentences
mode and subdivides overlong segments at internal punctuation: commas first,
then semicolons, colons, and space-separated en or em dashes.
.TP
.B words
Includes
.B punctuation
mode and also enables selected-language primary conjunction/clause words,
followed by fallback words and prepositions, then narrow connector repair.
This is the default.
.PP
For each long segment, the formatter uses the first enabled category that has a
safe fitting boundary, selecting its rightmost fitting candidate. When none
fits, it may select the nearest safe eligible boundary beyond the width. It
then repeats on the remainder. Punctuation remains on the preceding line;
word boundaries split before the word. Separate sentences are never joined to
fill a line. New breaks are checked against Markdown structure and rendering.
.SS Migration from earlier options
The previous defaults, with punctuation and both word categories enabled,
become
.BR --split-mode=words .
The former
.B --no-word-splitting
behavior becomes
.BR --split-mode=punctuation .
Sentence-only formatting is newly available as
.BR --split-mode=sentences .
The former primary-only combination
.B --word-splitting --no-extended
has no exact equivalent: words mode also enables fallback words, while
punctuation mode disables all word boundaries. The following old CLI options
are removed:
.BR --word-splitting ,
.BR --no-word-splitting ,
.BR --extended ,
.BR -e ,
.BR --no-extended .
The INI keys
.B word-splitting
and
.B extended
are removed too. Use the single
.B --split-mode
option or
.B split-mode
configuration key instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "-i, --infile " FILE
Read
.I FILE
instead of standard input. A relative path is resolved from the working
directory.
.TP
.BI "-o, --outfile " FILE
Write to
.I FILE
instead of standard output. The file is replaced atomically after successful
formatting and writing; the input may be the same path.
.TP
.BI "-w, --width " N
Set the positive source-character width. The default is 75. See the
.B Splitting modes
section and width rules above. Sentence mode ignores width when splitting
sentences.
.TP
.BI "-l, --languages " LIST
Select comma-separated language names or aliases, or
.B all
(the default). Canonical names are
.B english
and
.BR german ;
aliases are
.BR en ,
.BR eng ,
.BR de ,
.BR deu ,
and
.BR ger .
Selections are case-insensitive, deduplicated, and processed in canonical
order. Language selection affects word boundaries and abbreviation
recognition; it does not detect the input language.
.TP
.BI "--split-mode " MODE
Choose one cumulative mode. The default is
.BR words .
Accepted values and aliases are:
.RS
.TP
.B sentences
Aliases:
.BR sentence ,
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B punctuation
Aliases:
.BR comma ,
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B words
Aliases:
.BR word ,
.BR 3 .
.RE
.TP
.BI "--encoding " CODEC
Use the named text codec. The default
.B auto
detects UTF-8, UTF-16 LE/BE, or UTF-32 LE/BE from a byte-order mark (BOM), and
uses UTF-8 when no BOM is present. Other BOM-less encodings require an explicit
codec, for example
.BR latin-1 .
Decoding and encoding are strict. A detected BOM must agree with an explicit
codec; generic UTF-16 and UTF-32 require a matching BOM. The original BOM and
its presence or absence are preserved.
.TP
.BI "-c, --config-file " FILE
Use only this INI file instead of the default configuration search. A relative
path is resolved from the working directory. The file must contain a matching
section.
.TP
.BI "-s, --config-section " SECTION
Select this exact section rather than using the default or working-directory
path match. It applies to the configuration search unless
.B --config-file
is also supplied.
.TP
.B --list-languages
List canonical language names and exit.
.TP
.B --show-options
Print effective options and the selected INI section as JSON, then exit. This
validates configuration but does not read or format input.
.TP
.B --explain
Report selected formatting decisions and width exceptions on standard error.
Locations refer to final output lines. The formatted output is unchanged; with
file output, explanations are emitted only after the atomic replacement
succeeds. This is a command-line-only option, not an INI setting.
.TP
.B --version
Print the installed version and exit.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Print concise option help, cumulative-mode summaries, and examples, then exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration uses INI syntax with literal values. CLI values override the
selected section, which overrides built-in defaults. Files and sections are not
merged. Help and version do not require valid configuration; inspection
commands do not read input.
.PP
On macOS and Linux, pysembr searches
.I ./.sembr
then
.IR ~/.sembr .
On Windows, it searches
.I .\esembr.ini
then
.IR %APPDATA%\esembr\esembr.ini .
When
.B APPDATA
is unset, the second Windows location is
.IR ~/sembr.ini .
An explicitly selected
.B --config-file
replaces this search.
.PP
Without
.BR --config-section ,
the first matching section in file order is selected. A section named
.B [default]
matches, as does a normalized absolute path section containing the working
directory. The first matching section stops the search; a preceding
.B [default]
therefore takes precedence over a later path section. If no section matches in
one default candidate file, the next candidate is searched. The INI
.B [DEFAULT]
section can provide inherited values but does not itself select a section.
.PP
The ordinary keys mirror the CLI options:
.BR infile ,
.BR outfile ,
.BR width ,
.BR languages ,
.BR split-mode ,
.BR encoding ,
and
.BR list-languages .
Hyphens and underscores are interchangeable in keys. The inspection and
selection controls
.BR show-options ,
.BR config-file ,
and
.B config-section
are not INI settings. The
.B explain
option is command-line-only. Unknown or conflicting selected keys and invalid
values are errors. Values in unselected sections are not necessarily
validated.
.PP
The following six configuration-only keys replace the corresponding shipped
vocabulary list for one language:
.BR conjunctions-english ,
.BR conjunctions-german ,
.BR split-words-english ,
.BR split-words-german ,
.BR abbreviations-english ,
and
.BR abbreviations-german .
Values are comma-separated, stripped, and deduplicated without regard to case.
An empty value clears that list; a missing key retains the shipped list. Word
entries must be single words; abbreviations may contain spaces. These lists
affect the
.B words
mode, except abbreviation lists, which affect sentence recognition in every
mode. Overlapping word entries use the conjunction category's priority.
.PP
Example:
.nf
[default]
width = 60
languages = en,de
split-mode = punctuation
conjunctions-english = and, but, because
split_words_german = mit, ohne, zwischen
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./.sembr
First default configuration file on macOS and Linux.
.TP
.I ~/.sembr
Second default configuration file on macOS and Linux.
.TP
.I ./sembr.ini
First default configuration file on Windows.
.TP
.I %APPDATA%/sembr/sembr.ini
Second default configuration file on Windows when
.B APPDATA
is set.
.TP
.I ~/sembr.ini
Fallback second configuration file on Windows when
.B APPDATA
is unset.
.PP
Relative input and output file names are resolved from the working directory,
not from the configuration file's directory.
.SH FORMATTING AND DATA
.SS Markdown
The adopted dialect is CommonMark with pipe tables and native task lists,
plus definition lists, YAML front matter, footnotes, and dollar math.
Headings, code, block quotes, thematic breaks, HTML blocks, references,
tables, recognized front matter, footnote definitions, and block math are
protected. Whole links and images, inline code and HTML, math, footnote
references, and recognized bare URLs are protected inside prose. Protected
source is left as written. Hard breaks marked by two or more spaces or a
backslash, blank lines, and document structure are preserved. Soft-wrapped
prose lines are joined and ASCII spaces/tabs between prose words are
normalized. Unsafe Markdown boundaries are skipped; ambiguous affected prose
is preserved.
.SS Sentences and width
Recognized sentence endings include clusters of
.BR . ,
.BR ! ,
and
.BR ?
with adjacent closing quotes or brackets. Decimals, initials, selected
abbreviations, and punctuation inside protected spans do not create ordinary
sentence breaks. Abbreviation endings are ambiguous and are handled
conservatively. Existing hard breaks or document structure can still split a
sentence in
.B sentences
mode. Protected content and unbreakable segments may exceed width in any mode.
.SS Languages
English and German are enabled together by default. Split-word matching uses
the shipped reviewed inventories, selected languages, and any configured
replacements. Matching is case-insensitive and limited to whole words; it is
lexical rather than grammatical. Connector repair applies only in words mode.
It repairs exact standalone English fragments
.BR and ,
.BR but ,
and
.BR or ,
and German fragments
.BR und ,
.BR aber ,
and
.BR oder
when they belong to an enabled primary inventory. It joins a connector to the
following segment if that fits, otherwise to the preceding segment if that
fits. It never crosses a sentence or hard break.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
With neither file option, pysembr reads standard input and writes standard
output. Each file option independently takes precedence over its corresponding
stream. Relative paths are resolved from the working directory. Errors and
explanations go to standard error; explanations do not change formatted output.
.PP
For file output, pysembr reads the input completely, writes and closes an
exclusive random-suffix temporary file in the destination directory, then
uses path replacement to replace the destination. Input and output may be the
same path. A handled failure before replacement leaves the destination intact
and removes this run's temporary file. Standard output has ordinary stream
semantics, not atomic replacement semantics.
.SS Encoding and line endings
Input and output use binary streams so the selected encoding and original BOM
can be preserved. With
.B auto
and no BOM, input is UTF-8. Untouched source retains its original CRLF, CR, or
LF terminators. New lines in reformatted prose use that paragraph's first
terminator, otherwise the document's first terminator, otherwise LF. Mixed
endings outside reformatted prose remain unchanged. Final-newline presence,
blank-line counts, and empty input are preserved.
.SH EXAMPLES
Format standard input using each mode at width 25:
.nf
printf 'Alpha beta, gamma delta and epsilon zeta. Next.\en' | \\
    pysembr --split-mode sentences --width 25
printf 'Alpha beta, gamma delta and epsilon zeta. Next.\en' | \\
    pysembr --split-mode punctuation --width 25
printf 'Alpha beta, gamma delta and epsilon zeta. Next.\en' | \\
    pysembr --split-mode words --width 25
.fi
.PP
Run the same filter in a pipeline, or process a separate file:
.nf
cat input.md | pysembr --width 60 > output.md
pysembr --infile input.md --outfile output.md
.fi
.PP
Replace a file with its formatted version. Input is read before output
replacement, so both options may name the same file:
.nf
pysembr --infile note.md --outfile note.md
.fi
.PP
Explain decisions while keeping formatted text on standard output:
.nf
pysembr --explain < input.md > output.md 2> decisions.log
.fi
.PP
Inspect the effective configuration or list languages without formatting:
.nf
pysembr --show-options
pysembr --list-languages
.fi
.PP
To view the source-checkout manual locally, run
.BR "man ./docs/pysembr.1" .
The README also documents configuring Shellfilter in PyCharm and other
IntelliJ-based editors to filter a selected Markdown region.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Formatting or inspection completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Input, output, decoding, encoding, or formatting failed. A file destination
is left intact when a handled failure occurs before replacement.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line syntax, option values, or configuration are invalid.
.PP
Errors and explanations are written to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR README.md ,
in the source checkout for PyCharm and IntelliJ Shellfilter setup, and
